WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.467
Hello and welcome to mCoding.

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:05.919
Today we're talking about extending
 Python's built-in number system.

00:00:05.919 --> 00:00:10.840
We'll walk through a class that I've written
that implements infinite precision complex numbers.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.908
More specifically, this implementation models
what are called the Gaussian Rationals.

00:00:15.908 --> 00:00:20.533
These are just complex numbers where both
the real and imaginary parts are rational.

00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.691
As it turns out, implementing your own number
type is in some ways easier

00:00:23.691 --> 00:00:26.206
and in some ways harder than 
you might expect.

00:00:26.206 --> 00:00:28.703
Python provides a lot of machinery 
to help you do it.

00:00:28.703 --> 00:00:30.972
But there are also quite 
a few gotchas.

00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:37.100
In particular, making sure this class works with 
other existing number types like integers is a bit tricky.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.617
So let's dive in.

00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:42.629
The first issue we run into is what 
to even call this class.

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:45.303
Calling it a fraction is somewhat misleading.

00:00:45.303 --> 00:00:47.820
Any number can be written as a fraction.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.693
Pi, for instance, which is irrational, can
be written as pi divided by 1,

00:00:51.693 --> 00:00:53.350
which is a fraction.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.474
But Python already has a 
built-in fraction type

00:00:55.474 --> 00:00:59.035
which represents rational 
numbers, not fractions.

00:00:59.035 --> 00:01:01.323
So we'll steam ahead and call it 
a `ComplexFraction`.

00:01:01.323 --> 00:01:03.778
But be aware that naming is hard.

00:01:03.778 --> 00:01:08.115
The next thing to be aware of is that Python
already has a hierarchy of abstract base classes

00:01:08.115 --> 00:01:11.179
that represent different systems of numbers.

00:01:11.179 --> 00:01:15.580
Their number, complex, real, rational, and integral.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.972
With number being the base class
of all number types,

00:01:17.972 --> 00:01:22.555
even those that may represent things
 outside of the complex numbers like quaternions.

00:01:22.555 --> 00:01:24.710
Each of the others
just add on some operations

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:28.637
like complex numbers need to be able
 to convert the built-in complex.

00:01:28.637 --> 00:01:31.525
And they need to have real 
and imaginary parts.

00:01:31.525 --> 00:01:35.417
And they also tell you that you need to implement
things like addition and subtraction.

00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:38.930
The one that makes the most sense for
 us is to inherit from complex.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.735
There is also rational but that's not 
a good fit for our class.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:48.077
Internally our complex fraction is just going
to store two fractions: the real and imaginary parts.

00:01:48.077 --> 00:01:51.806
We don't want to allow storing any
 other attributes onto our class.

00:01:51.806 --> 00:01:56.790
So, we forced the real and imaginary parts 
are the only two things we can assign to using slots.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.766
This will also make our complex
 fractions use less memory.

00:01:59.766 --> 00:02:03.314
Next note that we're using
 `__new__` here, not `__init__`.

00:02:03.314 --> 00:02:08.430
That's because like all other built-in number types, 
we want our complex fraction to be immutable.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:12.296
Whenever you define a class that's immutable, you 
have to use `__new__` instead of `__init__`.

00:02:12.296 --> 00:02:17.765
Because although it would be uncommon, `__init__`
is allowed to be called on the same object multiple times.

00:02:17.765 --> 00:02:21.420
If `__init__` was the place that the real
and imaginary parts were set,

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.132
then that could potentially mutate an
 existing object which isn't allowed.

00:02:25.132 --> 00:02:30.162
Now, of course this is Python. So, there is no
such thing as true immutability.

00:02:30.162 --> 00:02:34.029
If someone really wanted to, they could reach
in and change this underscore attribute.

00:02:34.029 --> 00:02:36.348
But in Python, it's sort of 
an unspoken rule

00:02:36.348 --> 00:02:38.350
that if you have an attribute starting
 with an underscore,

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:42.791
it's an implementation detail that
shouldn't be accessed by the outside world.

00:02:42.791 --> 00:02:47.177
To give the outside world access to our
 real and imaginary parts, we just use properties.

00:02:47.177 --> 00:02:51.441
These properties allow anyone to read the
real or imaginary part like an attribute.

00:02:51.441 --> 00:02:54.257
But if they try to assign to it, 
they'd get an error.

00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:57.436
Also notice this little symbol 
on the left of my editor.

00:02:57.436 --> 00:03:00.416
This tells you that I'm overriding
 something in a base class.

00:03:00.416 --> 00:03:05.346
In this case, the real and imaginary properties
are required by our complex base class.

00:03:05.346 --> 00:03:09.560
Next up we have factories and
 conversion functions.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.153
It's really up to you to decide what kind of 
convenience functions that you want to give your users.

00:03:14.153 --> 00:03:17.922
Currently our `__new__` function takes
 the real and imaginary parts.

00:03:17.922 --> 00:03:20.888
We pass each part into 
the built-in fraction class.

00:03:20.888 --> 00:03:24.791
This means we're automatically supporting
anything that the built-in fraction does.

00:03:24.791 --> 00:03:28.873
In particular, we can handle rationals,
 floats, strings, and decimals.

00:03:28.873 --> 00:03:31.837
However, this doesn't cover 
all of the cases.

00:03:31.837 --> 00:03:36.752
For instance, we might like to be able to construct a 
complex fraction from a built-in complex number.

00:03:36.752 --> 00:03:40.286
Or from a string that contains both 
the real and imaginary parts.

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:43.705
So feel free to create whatever
 factories you think would be useful.

00:03:43.705 --> 00:03:47.161
And sometimes your users don't
 necessarily want to deal with your class.

00:03:47.161 --> 00:03:51.135
In this case, they might prefer to just have 
a tuple of the real and imaginary part.

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:55.222
And it's also good practice to give them
 a way to convert to the built-in complex type.

00:03:55.222 --> 00:03:58.038
This is also something that's 
required for a base class.

00:03:58.038 --> 00:04:03.926
Of course, to find a nice __str__ and
 __repr__ arithmetic operations.

00:04:03.926 --> 00:04:06.298
Addition, subtraction, and so on.

00:04:06.298 --> 00:04:09.180
And here's where the first big 
gotcha comes in.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.581
Why did I define this `_add` method

00:04:11.581 --> 00:04:16.127
instead of just making this the
 implementation of the `__add__` method?

00:04:16.127 --> 00:04:20.094
If you're not familiar with these double
 underscore or dunder methods,

00:04:20.094 --> 00:04:23.082
there are Python's way of allowing you 
to define for your own classes

00:04:23.082 --> 00:04:26.073
what things like plus, times, 
divide mean.

00:04:26.073 --> 00:04:32.556
So if I have two complex fractions, then `x + y` will call 
this `__add__` with `x` and `y` as arguments.

00:04:32.556 --> 00:04:38.097
And if we put our implementation in 
the `__add__` and set our `__radd__` to `None`,

00:04:38.097 --> 00:04:40.590
then our example appears to work.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:46.334
`1 + 2i + 3 + 4i` gives `4 + 6i`.

00:04:46.334 --> 00:04:50.690
But if you add a complex fraction 
to say a built-in complex type,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.074
then you might get 
an unexpected answer.

00:04:53.074 --> 00:04:58.402
What happened here is this `1.2` got 
converted into this crazy ratio of integers.

00:04:58.402 --> 00:05:01.320
Now maybe that is what you 
expected to happen.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.402
But what a lot of people would 
expect to happen

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:06.918
and what's in line with how some 
of the other built-in number types work,

00:05:06.918 --> 00:05:11.053
you might expect Python to recognize
 that the `y` here isn't a complex fraction.

00:05:11.053 --> 00:05:14.317
So, we should no longer try to 
be maintaining this infinite precision.

00:05:14.317 --> 00:05:18.354
and instead just convert our complex
 fraction to a normal complex.

00:05:18.354 --> 00:05:20.142
And do the addition there.

00:05:20.142 --> 00:05:22.783
This makes the result type a plain 
old complex number.

00:05:22.783 --> 00:05:25.377
And the result is much 
more readable.

00:05:25.377 --> 00:05:28.611
And there's a subtle other 
issue lurking here.

00:05:28.611 --> 00:05:32.140
Suppose we take `1 + 2i + 1`.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:37.108
Integers in Python are exact. So, we're happy 
to keep the answer as another complex fraction.

00:05:37.108 --> 00:05:41.718
But if instead of `x + y`, we do `y + x`.

00:05:41.718 --> 00:05:43.917
Now we get an error.

00:05:43.917 --> 00:05:48.172
When `x` was on the left-hand side, we
 were calling the complex fraction's `__add__` method.

00:05:48.172 --> 00:05:52.594
But when `y` is on the left-hand side,
 we're calling the `__add__` method of the built-in int.

00:05:52.594 --> 00:05:56.245
The built-in int does not know how to
 add itself to our complex fraction.

00:05:56.245 --> 00:05:58.167
Resulting in the error.

00:05:58.167 --> 00:06:01.169
And this is the purpose of 
our `__radd__`.

00:06:01.169 --> 00:06:05.652
`__radd__` is supposed to do the same thing as 
`__add__` but with the arguments in the reverse order.

00:06:05.652 --> 00:06:10.260
If the built-in int indicates that it doesn't
 know how to add itself to the other object,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.687
Python will then go to the other object

00:06:12.687 --> 00:06:16.887
and ask itself if it knows how to
 reverse add itself to the int.

00:06:16.887 --> 00:06:19.900
So `__add__` gets called when self 
is on the left-hand side.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:25.869
And `__radd__` gets called if it can't use
 others' add and self is on the right-hand side.

00:06:25.869 --> 00:06:29.395
The implementation though is exactly the same as the other add

00:06:29.395 --> 00:06:31.256
just with the argument swapped.

00:06:31.256 --> 00:06:34.639
In this case, addition is commutative.
 So, we didn't have to swap them.

00:06:34.639 --> 00:06:39.583
But in general, like, if you were implementing division,
 then swapping them is the right thing to do.

00:06:39.583 --> 00:06:43.744
Now we'd like to solve both of our
 problems at the same time.

00:06:43.744 --> 00:06:47.552
We have repeated code that's in need
 of a shared implementation.

00:06:47.552 --> 00:06:51.000
And we want to implement this behavior of 
falling back to floating-point arithmetic

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.589
if we can't do exact operations.

00:06:53.589 --> 00:06:57.283
So we introduce our `_add` method.

00:06:57.283 --> 00:07:02.528
We can now implement the `__add__`
 and `__radd__` methods in terms of `_add`,

00:07:02.528 --> 00:07:06.323
just adding self other versus 
adding other self.

00:07:06.323 --> 00:07:09.566
Then we need to add the fallback 
logic into both methods.

00:07:09.566 --> 00:07:12.911
Now if we just had add an radd, we
 might just do that manually.

00:07:12.911 --> 00:07:14.707
But because we're going to 
need to do this logic

00:07:14.707 --> 00:07:19.187
for +, --, x, /,  and any other arithmetic
 operations that you would need,

00:07:19.187 --> 00:07:23.162
instead we're going to define a function
 which generates both of those functions for us.

00:07:23.162 --> 00:07:26.112
And that's what this operator 
fallbacks does.

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:30.991
It takes in the operation that we want to do in the
 case that we can handle everything in infinite precision.

00:07:30.991 --> 00:07:35.446
And it also takes in the operation that we want
 to do if it has to fall back to floating-point arithmetic.

00:07:35.446 --> 00:07:40.458
In this case, we're just using `operator.add`, 
which is the built-in add operation.

00:07:40.458 --> 00:07:43.490
So how does this operator 
fallbacks work?

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.736
Well, the good news is that the Python
 documentation actually gives us

00:07:46.736 --> 00:07:49.812
kind of a reference 
implementation of what to do.

00:07:49.812 --> 00:07:54.685
All I did was take the function in the docs.
 And change some of the types here and there.

00:07:54.685 --> 00:08:00.394
For the forward pass, we check to see if the 
other argument has a type that we specifically support.

00:08:00.394 --> 00:08:05.450
In our case, that's any other exact
 rational number or another complex fraction.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:09.771
Otherwise, we check to see if we
 should fall back to floating-point arithmetic.

00:08:09.771 --> 00:08:14.221
The way that Python lets you signal to
 let the other argument try its reverse method

00:08:14.221 --> 00:08:16.636
is by returning `NotImplemented`

00:08:16.636 --> 00:08:19.421
or if this function wasn't defined at all.

00:08:19.421 --> 00:08:27.485
The integers `__add__` would return `NotImplemented` to 
allow our complex fraction's `__radd__` to be called.

00:08:27.485 --> 00:08:32.095
Instead of `ab`, we use `ba` in 
the reverse version.

00:08:32.095 --> 00:08:36.925
This is so we can keep the logic mostly
 the same but still end up with swapped arguments.

00:08:36.925 --> 00:08:40.063
Once again, we check for things 
that we specifically support.

00:08:40.063 --> 00:08:42.853
Otherwise, we check if the other 
thing is a complex number

00:08:42.853 --> 00:08:46.151
and fallback to floating 
point in that case.

00:08:46.151 --> 00:08:50.118
Notice however that these cases are
 not exactly the same though.

00:08:50.118 --> 00:08:53.590
Here we're checking against specific
 types: float and complex.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:57.013
Whereas here we're checking with 
the abstract interface complex.

00:08:57.013 --> 00:09:00.529
In the forward pass, we don't want to
 check against an abstract interface here

00:09:00.529 --> 00:09:02.575
because that would be a little 
bit too eager.

00:09:02.575 --> 00:09:06.000
We wouldn't want to immediately fall
 back to floating-point arithmetic

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.403
if `b` happened to implement 
the complex interface.

00:09:09.403 --> 00:09:13.117
For all we know, `b` is another kind of
 exact complex number type

00:09:13.117 --> 00:09:16.162
that knows about our complex 
number type.

00:09:16.162 --> 00:09:20.699
So we would want to give that type a
 chance to do its reverse operation.

00:09:20.699 --> 00:09:25.230
We only fall back in the forward pass if we're 
sure there's nothing more intelligent we can do.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.380
I really encourage just reading 
the documentation here.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:31.955
I don't think explaining it in a video is
 the right way to catch all the subtleties.

00:09:31.955 --> 00:09:38.394
But in any case, we define the forward and 
reverse versions of our operations and then return them.

00:09:38.394 --> 00:09:41.133
Now we understand why it's 
written this way.

00:09:41.133 --> 00:09:45.411
We factored out the functionality of all of the 
cases that we know how to add into this function.

00:09:45.411 --> 00:09:50.588
otherwise, fall back to the default operation after 
converting to a floating-point number.

00:09:50.588 --> 00:09:55.912
Repeat for subtraction, multiplication, 
and division.

00:09:55.912 --> 00:10:00.635
Unfortunately, we have to write exponentiation 
for ourselves, both forward and reverse.

00:10:00.635 --> 00:10:04.974
This is because unlike with addition, 
multiplication, subtraction, and division,

00:10:04.974 --> 00:10:10.456
a complex fraction to the power of a complex 
fraction is not necessarily another complex fraction.

00:10:10.456 --> 00:10:16.085
So we basically just fall back to floating-point 
arithmetic unless we have an integer exponent.

00:10:16.085 --> 00:10:18.535
Handle the case of 0 separately.

00:10:18.535 --> 00:10:22.660
If our exponent was negative,
 then do one over the absolute value.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:26.190
And if we got a positive integer, 
 then used _fast_pow.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.760
I just made a video on _fast_pow. So if
 you want to hear more about it, check it out.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.309
But suffice it to say that it worked 
with complex numbers.

00:10:33.309 --> 00:10:39.095
For the reverse version, we're raising some base 
to the power of ourselves which is a complex number.

00:10:39.095 --> 00:10:43.627
So we're pretty much always going to have to fall
 back to floating-point arithmetic in that case.

00:10:43.627 --> 00:10:47.251
We do make one special exception 
where if we happen to be purely real,

00:10:47.251 --> 00:10:51.274
the built-in fraction class that is our real 
part might have something more sensible to do.

00:10:51.274 --> 00:10:53.354
Although honestly, probably not.

00:10:53.354 --> 00:10:57.533
Positive and negative are pretty 
self-explanatory.

00:10:57.533 --> 00:11:01.838
For absolute value, we separate out the
 case where we're purely real or purely imaginary.

00:11:01.838 --> 00:11:04.061
In that case, we can give an 
exact answer.

00:11:04.061 --> 00:11:06.912
Otherwise, we do square root of a
 squared plus b squared.

00:11:06.912 --> 00:11:10.043
The a squared plus b squared part 
is this norm squared.

00:11:10.043 --> 00:11:11.969
This isn't required by the 
complex interface.

00:11:11.969 --> 00:11:15.565
But oftentimes you can use norm 
squared instead of using absolute value.

00:11:15.565 --> 00:11:21.914
If so, that's a big win because the norm squared is always 
going to be an exact infinite precision fraction.

00:11:21.914 --> 00:11:25.710
Whereas the absolute value typically
 is not because of the square root.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:27.213
Then we come down to conjugate.

00:11:27.213 --> 00:11:30.136
This is required by the interface and 
it's fairly straightforward.

00:11:30.136 --> 00:11:33.086
Just flip the sign of the 
imaginary part.

00:11:33.086 --> 00:11:39.087
Then we come to probably the most 
subtle and confusing part: the hash function.

00:11:39.087 --> 00:11:43.786
The first time I implemented this class,
 I didn't realize how subtle this was.

00:11:43.786 --> 00:11:45.567
I thought, "Okay, I need 
a hash function.

00:11:45.567 --> 00:11:48.944
Why don't I just take the hash of the
 pair of the real and imaginary parts?"

00:11:48.944 --> 00:11:51.144
Simple enough, right?

00:11:51.144 --> 00:11:53.064
Well, that's no good.

00:11:53.064 --> 00:11:58.174
The reason that's no good is that I want my 
complex fraction to work like all the other number types.

00:11:58.174 --> 00:12:01.535
In particular, complex fraction 
zero should be equal to zero.

00:12:01.535 --> 00:12:05.109
It should be equal to 0.0. 
It should be equal to the fraction zero;

00:12:05.109 --> 00:12:08.088
And it should be equal to the 
complex zero.

00:12:08.088 --> 00:12:12.623
Zeros of all the other types compare
 equal to the integer zero.

00:12:12.623 --> 00:12:18.012
And more generally, whenever two numbers 
of different types represent the same theoretical value,

00:12:18.012 --> 00:12:19.734
they still compare equal.

00:12:19.734 --> 00:12:21.527
Although, due to rounding errors, of course,

00:12:21.527 --> 00:12:26.653
it doesn't always work out the way 
that we might think based off of the notation.

00:12:26.653 --> 00:12:29.443
Okay, so this might complicate our
 equality function.

00:12:29.443 --> 00:12:32.268
But what does this have to do 
with the hash?

00:12:32.268 --> 00:12:37.978
In order for sets and dictionaries to work, 
equal values must have equal hashes.

00:12:37.978 --> 00:12:42.918
That means that if I want my complex
 fraction to compare equal to other numbers,

00:12:42.918 --> 00:12:47.330
it needs to have the same hash as 
those numbers if they represent the same value.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:51.501
Whether it's zero, float zero, complex
 zero or complex fraction zero,

00:12:51.501 --> 00:12:55.061
they all need to have the same hash: 
the hash of zero.

00:12:55.061 --> 00:12:56.783
Checking equality is very simple.

00:12:56.783 --> 00:12:59.643
If I see something else implementing
 the complex interface,

00:12:59.643 --> 00:13:02.547
then I just compare real and 
imaginary parts.

00:13:02.547 --> 00:13:05.754
But how do you come up with 
this hash function?

00:13:05.754 --> 00:13:09.706
Well, once again it's read 
the documentation to the rescue.

00:13:09.707 --> 00:13:12.000
I don't have to come up with 
my own hash function.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:14.028
The documentation essentially says:

00:13:14.028 --> 00:13:17.677
if you're implementing a complex
 number type, use this hash function.

00:13:17.677 --> 00:13:21.384
I didn't come up with it. I'm just 
using the one that I found in the docs.

00:13:21.384 --> 00:13:23.808
At first, I didn't even realize 
there was an issue.

00:13:23.808 --> 00:13:28.362
Then I thought it was a huge issue. 
And then the docs just gave me the answer.

00:13:28.362 --> 00:13:32.644
It was kind of a roller coaster ride to go on. 
But I'm glad to know that it's been sorted out.

00:13:32.644 --> 00:13:38.710
Moving on we finish up with the boolean conversion operator 
`__reduce__`, `__copy__` and `__deepcopy__`.

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:43.102
We actually didn't need to implement this one. 
It's actually included in our base class.

00:13:43.102 --> 00:13:46.621
Remember zero is false; 
non-zero values are true.

00:13:46.621 --> 00:13:48.695
`__reduce__` is for pickling.

00:13:48.695 --> 00:13:51.343
For `__reduce__` we return two things.

00:13:51.343 --> 00:13:55.391
The first is a callable that we'll use to
 reconstruct the object when we unpickle.

00:13:55.391 --> 00:13:57.070
And the second is the state.

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.997
So we essentially pickle the real 
and imaginary parts separately.

00:14:00.997 --> 00:14:05.293
And then reconstruct it by passing 
it to the class to create a new instance.

00:14:05.293 --> 00:14:09.044
`__copy__` and `__deepcopy__` are kind of 
interesting since they just return self.

00:14:09.044 --> 00:14:11.201
Remember our class is immutable.

00:14:11.201 --> 00:14:13.575
So how do we make a copy of
 something immutable?

00:14:13.575 --> 00:14:16.781
There's no need to actually make a
 copy because we're immutable.

00:14:16.781 --> 00:14:18.596
So, we just return ourselves.

00:14:18.596 --> 00:14:20.210
We do need to be careful though.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:24.871
If someone subclasses our class, 
they might not want it to be immutable anymore.

00:14:24.871 --> 00:14:27.857
So if the type isn't exactly 
complex fraction,

00:14:27.857 --> 00:14:32.058
Then we should just pass the real and
 imaginary parts and try to construct a new one.

00:14:32.058 --> 00:14:33.201
Same thing for `__deepcopy__`.

00:14:33.201 --> 00:14:37.815
Our real and imaginary parts are
 themselves both fractions which are immutable.

00:14:37.815 --> 00:14:41.415
So, there's nothing to deep copy 
and we just return self.

00:14:41.415 --> 00:14:46.194
And with all that we now have our 
own infinite precision complex fraction type!

00:14:46.194 --> 00:14:50.587
Of course, don't forget to write tests.
 Lots and lots of tests.

00:14:50.587 --> 00:14:55.203
Especially involving hashes and 
make sure dictionaries work properly.

00:14:55.203 --> 00:14:59.594
I also wanted to point out one thing
 that I didn't talk about and that's comparisons.

00:14:59.594 --> 00:15:01.635
Complex numbers don't have 
a natural order to them.

00:15:01.635 --> 00:15:03.774
So I didn't define any of 
the less than,

00:15:03.774 --> 00:15:07.136
less than or equal to, greater than, 
or greater than or equal to methods.

00:15:07.136 --> 00:15:09.873
Since they weren't defined, 
we'll just get type errors.

00:15:09.873 --> 00:15:15.085
But if you were modeling a subset of the real numbers, 
you probably would want to define them.

